  "body": {
    "id": "thm:null-supported-converse-fails",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For \\(d=\\gamma=1\\), \\(\\alpha=1/10\\), \\(\\beta=9/10\\), and every fixed \\(L\\ge2\\), there are constants \\(0<c<C<\\infty\\) and \\(n_0\\) such that the deterministic mixture optimization satisfies \\(r_{\\mathrm{mix}}(n)\\ge c n^{-5/14}\\), whereas \\(\\rho_n^*\\le Cn^{-2/5}\\), for every \\(n\\ge n_0\\). Consequently no fixed \\(c_{\\mathrm{lb}}>0\\) can make \\(\\rho_n^*\\ge c_{\\mathrm{lb}}r_{\\mathrm{mix}}(n)\\); in particular, the universal null-supported prior construction requested in the source question does not exist for the frozen feasible set and divergence envelope.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "d",
      "gamma",
      "alpha",
      "beta",
      "L",
      "rMix",
      "n",
      "rhoStar",
      "cLB"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:mixture-index-set",
      "def:mixture-separation",
      "def:mixture-divergence-envelope",
      "def:mixture-optimization",
      "prop:single-scale-upper",
      "def:observational-critical-radius"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Fix the displayed parameter values and let \\(a=2^{-9}\\). For sufficiently large \\(n\\), set\n\\[\n M_n=\\lceil n^{5/14}\\rceil,\n \\qquad \\kappa_{\\mathrm{mix}}=\\frac18,\n \\qquad q_e=q_b=0,\n\\]\ntake \\(K=1,w_1=1,u_1=0\\), so \\(\\nu_\\theta=\\delta_0\\), and let \\(q_\\tau\\) be the largest point of \\(G_n\\) not exceeding \\(a/M_n\\). Since the mesh of \\(G_n\\) is \\(1/(64n^2)\\) and \\(M_n=o(n^2)\\),\n\\[\n \\frac{a}{2M_n}\\le q_\\tau\\le\\frac{a}{M_n}                            \\tag{34}\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(n\\). The tuple is in \\(\\Theta_n\\): the zero amplitudes meet their restrictions; \\(\\delta_0\\in\\mathcal N_n(0)\\); \\(A_\\theta=q_\\tau\\le1/32\\); and, because \\(1-2\\kappa_{\\mathrm{mix}}=3/4\\),\n\\[\n q_\\tau\\le\\frac{a}{M_n}\n \\le\\frac{3L}{256M_n}\n =\\frac{L}{64}\\left(\\frac{1-2\\kappa_{\\mathrm{mix}}}{M_n}\\right)^\\gamma.\n\\]\nFor this tuple \\(\\zeta_2=\\zeta_3=\\zeta_4=0\\), hence \\(R_2=R_3=R_4=0\\). The frozen divergence formula reduces to\n\\[\n E_n(\\theta)\n =2^{16}\\frac{(n)_5M_n^{-4}q_\\tau^{10}}{1-16q_\\tau}\n \\le2^{17}a^{10}n^5M_n^{-14}\n \\le2^{17}a^{10}<\\log2.                                              \\tag{35}\n\\]\nIt follows from the definition of \\(V_n\\) that \\(V_n(\\theta)<1/2=3/4-a_0-b_0\\), so this tuple is feasible in the maximum defining \\(r_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\). Put\n\\[\n c_b=\\left\\{\\frac34\\mu_2-\\frac9{16}\\mu_1^2\\right\\}^{1/2}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mu_1^2\\le\\mu_2\\) and the nonconstant nonnegative bump is not zero, \\(c_b^2\\ge3\\mu_2/16>0\\). Equations (34) and the definition of \\(\\Delta_n\\) now give\n\\[\n r_{\\mathrm{mix}}(n)\\ge\\Delta_n(\\theta)=c_bq_\\tau\n \\ge c n^{-5/14},\\qquad c:=ac_b/4>0.                                 \\tag{36}\n\\]\nOn the other hand, the already proved single-scale upper bound has \\(t=1\\) and \\(d=1\\le4t\\), so\n\\[\n \\rho_n^*\\le Cn^{-2/5}.                                               \\tag{37}\n\\]\nThe ratio between the right side of (36) and the right side of (37) grows as \\(n^{2/5-5/14}=n^{3/70}\\). Thus \\(\\rho_n^*\\ge c_{\\mathrm{lb}}r_{\\mathrm{mix}}(n)\\) is impossible for every fixed positive \\(c_{\\mathrm{lb}}\\).\n\nFinally, the requested priors would imply precisely that impossible inequality. Indeed, at every feasible point with \\(V_n\\le1/2\\), a level-\\(a_0\\) test with type-II error at most \\(b_0\\) would make its rejection expectations under the two prior-predictive laws differ by at least \\(1-a_0-b_0=3/4\\), while total variation would bound that difference by \\(V_n\\le1/2\\). Applying this argument to a maximizer in the finite definition of \\(r_{\\mathrm{mix}}\\) yields the contradicted lower bound. Therefore the source question has the asserted negative answer.",
